WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.456
Raj: A cache hit restores local configuration. That file contains the retired address.

2
00:00:06.456 --> 00:00:13.056
Priya: Okay, is this cache poisoning then? Or just stale configuration carried forward?

3
00:00:13.056 --> 00:00:19.968
Lukas: Don't label it malicious without evidence. Compare cache creation records and access logs.

4
00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:26.472
Anika: The dependency mirror changed last Tuesday. This cache predates that change, right?

5
00:00:26.472 --> 00:00:33.720
Hannah: Yes, its writer was an approved runner. No unauthorized writes found so far.

6
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:40.296
Raj: Cold runs pass; restored runs fail. I've preserved the cache object digest.

7
00:00:40.296 --> 00:00:47.928
Lukas: That's useful paired evidence, not certainty. Why configuration was cached still needs investigation.

8
00:00:47.928 --> 00:00:53.976
Priya: Just to confirm, quarantine that object. The root cause is still open.

9
00:00:53.976 --> 00:01:00.888
Hannah: I'll keep an eye on access. Cache inspection mustn't reveal embedded credentials.

10
00:01:00.888 --> 00:01:07.800
Anika: Understood, restricted inspection rather than downloads. Maybe cache scope was too broad.

